NAME
    jsc - compile the original ordered JavaScript namespaces or CSS sources

SYNOPSIS
    jsc <modules.json> [output]

DESCRIPTION
    jsc compiles the original m source description format. Its input is an
    ordered JSON tree containing namespace nodes (name/modules) and source
    nodes (file/symbols). No module IDs, dependency lists, build graph or
    separate runtime registry are required.

    Earlier declarations are available to later source files in the same
    lexical namespace. Symbols exported in a nested namespace become
    accessible through the corresponding namespace object in parent scopes.

    jsc uses the default m-managed Node.js runtime through the existing
    shell bootstrap. It does not install Node.js automatically.

COMPLETE EXAMPLE
    Given these files:

        project/
          modules-js.json
          m/srv/data/MyClass.js
          m/srv/data/Other.js
          m/srv/Facade.js

    Contents of m/srv/data/MyClass.js:

        function MyClass() {
          return 42;
        }

    Contents of m/srv/data/Other.js:

        function Other() {
          return MyClass();
        }

    Contents of m/srv/Facade.js:

        function Facade() {
          return data.MyClass() + data.Other() - 42;
        }

    Contents of modules-js.json (order matters):

        {
          "modules": [
            {
              "name": "m",
              "modules": [
                {
                  "name": "srv",
                  "modules": [
                    {
                      "name": "data",
                      "modules": [
                        {"file": "m/srv/data/MyClass.js", "symbols": "MyClass"},
                        {"file": "m/srv/data/Other.js", "symbols": "Other"}
                      ]
                    },
                    {"file": "m/srv/Facade.js", "symbols": "Facade"}
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }

    Compile to standard output:

        jsc modules-js.json > bundle.js

    Or compile directly to a file:

        jsc modules-js.json bundle.js

    In a browser, load bundle.js:

        <!doctype html>
        <meta charset="utf-8">
        <script src="bundle.js"></script>
        <body>
          <div id="result"></div>
          <script>
            console.log(m.srv.data.MyClass()); // 42
            console.log(m.srv.data.Other());   // 42
            document.getElementById('result').textContent =
              String(m.srv.Facade());
          </script>

    The result is 42. The standalone classic script creates namespace m,
    m.srv and m.srv.data as it executes. In the data namespace, MyClass is
    directly usable by later source files; in m.srv, data.MyClass is usable;
    qualified access through m.srv.data.MyClass is also available.

MODULES.JSON FORMAT
    The root object contains an ordered "modules" array.

    A namespace node:
        name       namespace name: one valid JavaScript identifier
        modules    nested ordered array of namespace and source nodes

    A source node:
        file       relative source pathname
        symbols    optional comma-separated exported identifiers

    Example of multiple exports:

        {"file": "m/util/clock.js", "symbols": "clock,clockUTC"}

    Example of a side-effect-only source:

        {"file": "m/init.js", "symbols": ""}

    The files are included in JSON order. jsc does not automatically sort
    them, discover references or build a dependency graph. The project
    author chooses order. Source paths are relative to the directory of
    the manifest, rather than the shell working directory. Sources must
    resolve inside that directory. Unknown record members, unsafe names
    and invalid output syntax are rejected.

    Namespace objects can be reopened by a subsequently loaded bundle.
    Previous exported namespace members remain accessible when doing so.

CSS
    A CSS manifest is another ordered source list, without namespaces:

        {
          "modules": [
            {"file": "css/reset.css"},
            {"file": "css/layout.css"}
          ]
        }

    Example:

        jsc modules-css.json styles.css

    Concatenation respects input order. JavaScript and CSS belong in
    different manifests and are not mixed inside one assembly.

OUTPUT AND RELEASE ARTIFACTS
    With one operand, source bytes are emitted only to stdout. Redirect
    this stream to produce your file; no additional files are created.

    With an explicit output filename, jsc writes the assembled file.
    It additionally produces gzip output. When an already-installed
    compatible minifier is available, it produces <name>.min.js or
    <name>.min.css and the corresponding .gz of that minified file.

    JavaScript minification uses an installed google-closure-compiler.
    CSS minification uses an installed uglifycss. If the minifier is
    absent, the raw assembly remains usable and its .gz is produced
    with a warning. jsc does not install tools or access the network.

    Syntax/input validation is performed before replacing output.
    A filesystem write failure is not an atomic rollback guarantee.

ERRORS
    Errors are reported with distinct jsc.<message-id> diagnostics and
    nonzero statuses. The m launcher uses log/fatal for errors occurring
    before Node starts; the Node compiler reports its own errors without
    generating a duplicate shell fatal.

    Major compiler statuses:
         1   Invalid operands
         2   Manifest unreadable
         3   No sources declared
         4   Source pathname unresolved
         7   Invalid assembled JavaScript
         8   Output cannot be written
         9   Unexpected compiler failure
        11   Invalid manifest JSON
        12   Invalid manifest shape
        20   Source outside manifest directory
        21   Unreadable source file
        23   Invalid namespace or symbol identifier
        24   Invalid source/namespace record
        25   Mixed JS/CSS or CSS namespace
        26   Duplicate export symbol
        27   Installed minifier failed
        28   Gzip output failed

    Launcher statuses:
         1   Missing/invalid operands
         2   Compiler engine unavailable
         3   Default m-managed nodejs runtime unavailable
         4   Node executable unavailable

